 "xml": "<?xml version=\"1.0\" encoding=\"utf-8\" ?><transcript><text start=\"0.4\" dur=\"4.2\">hi everyone welcome back to the shed</text><text start=\"3.1\" dur=\"4.2\">today it&amp;#39;s home batteries</text><text start=\"6.9\" dur=\"4.2\">and whether one big one or two small ones is better</text><text start=\"15.2\" dur=\"4.2\">first what a home battery actually does</text><text start=\"19.8\" dur=\"4.2\">it stores the solar you make at noon</text><text start=\"24.5\" dur=\"4.2\">so you can use it at teatime</text><text start=\"62\" dur=\"4.2\">the cheap overnight tariff is the second trick</text><text start=\"66.3\" dur=\"4.2\">charge from the grid at 7p and use it at 28p</text><text start=\"181.5\" dur=\"4.2\">now the efficiency numbers</text><text start=\"186\" dur=\"4.2\">about ten percent is lost going in and out</text><text start=\"302.2\" dur=\"4.2\">here is the bit people miss</text><text start=\"306.7\" dur=\"4.2\">a battery has a limit on how fast it can discharge</text><text start=\"455\" dur=\"4.2\">so with one battery the kettle and oven together</text><text start=\"459.4\" dur=\"4.2\">pull from the grid anyway</text><text start=\"760.3\" dur=\"4.2\">why the second battery matters</text><text start=\"764.9\" dur=\"4.2\">two batteries double the power you can draw at once</text><text start=\"905\" dur=\"4.2\">what it costs and the payback</text><text start=\"910.2\" dur=\"4.2\">roughly eight to ten years on the tariffs today</text><text start=\"1080\" dur=\"4.2\">so my advice</text><text start=\"1085.5\" dur=\"4.2\">size for your evening power not just your energy</text><text start=\"1100.1\" dur=\"4.2\">thanks for watching</text></transcript>",
